# Calculate yearly stats across movies here
yearly_summary = (
    movies.groupby('year')['worldwide_gross']
    .agg(avg_revenue='mean', deviation='std')
    .sort_values('year', ascending=True)
)

#print(yearly_summary)

plt.close()
plt.figure()
plt.plot(yearly_summary.index, yearly_summary['avg_revenue'], marker='o', color='r')
plt.xlabel('Year')
plt.ylabel('Average Worldwide Gross')
plt.title('Average Revenue by Year')
plt.show()


# Write your code to make plot with errorbars here
plt.figure()
plt.errorbar(
    yearly_summary.index, yearly_summary['avg_revenue'],
    yerr=yearly_summary['deviation'],
    marker='o', capsize=4
)
plt.xlabel('Year')
plt.ylabel('Average Worldwide Gross')
plt.title('Average Revenue by Year with Error Bars')
plt.show()


# Write your code to make filled plot here
plt.figure()
plt.plot(yearly_summary.index, yearly_summary['avg_revenue'], marker='o', label='Average Revenue')

plt.fill_between(
    yearly_summary.index,
    yearly_summary['avg_revenue'] - yearly_summary['deviation'],
    yearly_summary['avg_revenue'] + yearly_summary['deviation'],
    color='grey',
    alpha=0.3,
    label='± 1 std dev'
)

plt.xlabel('Year')
plt.ylabel('Average Worldwide Gross')
plt.title('Average Revenue by Year with Standard Deviation')
plt.legend()
plt.show()


## Write your interpretation here

1998 and 2009 had the biggest deviations. Revenue kept increasing, which meakes sense because of inflation. 
But then it drops again. This could be due to a skewing effect caused by a large number of low grossing films.





